    "case_id": "676",
    "case_summary": "A 44-year-old man with a history of schizophrenia was admitted to the psychiatric Department. The patient was under antipsychotic treatment (risperidone) with antiparkinsonian drug (Biperiden) without treatment withdrawal.\n\n- Consciousness was disturbed, with a score of 7 on the Glasgow scale- Fever of 38-39°C- Tachycardia- Presented with rigidity, negativism, mutism, catalepsy, waxy flexibility and stupor- Rated 25/69 according to Bush-Francis Catatonia Rating Scale\n\n- Laboratory findings:  * Elevated creatine phosphokinase (CPK)  * Elevated liver enzymes  * Leucocytosis  * No metabolic disorders  * No hyponatremia or hypoglycemia  * Unremarkable urinary cytobacteriological examination- Imaging Studies:  * Chest X-ray: Unremarkable  * Cerebral CT: Unremarkable  * Brain MRI findings:    Image Title: Brain MR Images    Image Description: Round lesion in the splenium of the corpus callosum, hyperintense on FLAIR and T2-weighted images, hypointense on T1-weighted images, no enhancement after gadolinium administration, with restricted diffusion suggesting cytotoxic edema      Follow-up MRI:    Image Description: Complete resolution of the lesion in the splenium of the corpus callosum",
